(window.__CLAIMS_ADD__||function(){})([{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F1: OEWS wage-and-salary graphic designers 211,890 (May 2022) to 197,830 (May 2025), -6.6%. Checked against BLS's own files, with the in-between years: 212,720 (2023) and 214,260 (2024). The 2025 standard error is 2.4%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"(same claim, May 2023 point, U52)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1b"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"(same claim, May 2025 point)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a1c"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U38/F1 said the May 2024 designer count of 214,260 \"comes only from an Adobe marketing page\". It is in BLS's own May 2024 national file, so it is primary. The saved Adobe page shows only the May 2025 count, not 214,260. That makes the drop real and recent: -7.7% in one year, May 2024 to May 2025.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F1: graphic designer median pay $57,990 (2022) to $62,960 (2025), +8.6% nominal. Checked: hourly median $27.88 to $30.27 (+8.6%); the 2025 annual median is $62,960. The 2022 annual figure is the hourly median times 2,080, which is BLS's convention.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F1: broadcast announcers and radio DJs 26,820 (2022) to 21,240 (2025), -21%, with median hourly pay $20.07 to $22.76. Checked: 2023 was 25,070 and 2024 was 23,880, a steady slide.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F1 scriptTrap (the fine-artists cross-check): fine artists/illustrators 12,080 (2022) to 11,220 (2025), \"-7%\". The 11,220 is correct, but the series is 12,080, 10,910, 10,000, then 11,220: it ROSE 12% from 2024 to 2025. The 2022 figure has a 13.4% standard error. There is no measurable decline; do not present this row as a shrinking job.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F2 (flagged suspect): salaried animators 35,990 (2022) to 29,940 (2023) to 19,970 (2025). The counts are right and the 2025 median of $49.06 matches. But F2's reading that \"much of the fall had already happened by May 2023\" is wrong. The full series is 35,990, 29,940, 21,280 (2024), 19,970. Only 6,050 of the 16,020 drop (38%) came by May 2023. The biggest step was May 2023 to May 2024 (-8,660). The arithmetic drop is 44.5%, still not airable as \"45%\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"(F2, the 2025 count in the news release)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a6b"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F2: about 63% of animators are self-employed, and the all-workers count \"shows 51,900 in 2025, nearly flat\". The 63% is confirmed (62.9% in the matrix). But \"nearly flat\" (51.9k to 51.8k) is the 2025-35 PROJECTION, not past history. The previous projections edition put the 2024 all-workers base at 57,100, according to Adobe's page (secondary; the BLS original was not reached). If that is right, the all-workers count also fell about 9% in a year. Editions can revise their bases, so treat that as indicative only.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F1 scriptTrap: OEWS counts only wage-and-salary jobs, not the self-employed. Confirmed. A further caveat the dossier missed: each May estimate pools six survey panels over three years, so neighbouring years share most of their data and a single year's change is smoothed and lagged.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F3: 2025-35 projections (all workers, including the self-employed). Graphic designers 253.1k to 248.8k (-1.7%); animators 51.9k to 51.8k (-0.3%); fine artists 28.9k to 28.0k (-2.9%); announcers 21.8k to 20.2k (-7.6%); actors flat at 67.5k.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U67/U102: the Handbook says designers are projected to decline 2% from 2025 to 2035 and names AI as a reason, while still expecting designers to be needed for layouts and branding.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F3 said the prior edition's \"+2% for 2024-34\" was unverified. It is now confirmed from the archived Handbook page (Dec 2025). That edition's AI line was narrower: AI might cut the need to contract FREELANCE designers. Its 2024 all-workers base was 265,900, against 253,100 in the 2025 edition.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U102: for craft and fine artists, the Handbook says AI illustrations may compete with illustrators and are projected to reduce employment in that subset.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U102: for animators, the Handbook says some tasks can be done faster with AI, which may dampen demand.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U102: for announcers, the only AI mention is radio stations running AI DJs; consolidation is the lead reason.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U6: BLS's 50-year review of the Handbook. Linotype, monotype and hand-composing jobs mostly disappeared; the three were last described in the 1986-87 edition; desktop-publishing workers replaced them; by 1998-99 customers did layout on their own computers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U22/F4: Hui, Reshef and Zhou, Upwork. After ChatGPT, writing-related freelancers' monthly jobs fell 2% and monthly earnings fell 5.2%. Confirmed in the paper's own text (SSRN working paper, July 2023). The tables of the published Organization Science version were not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U23/F4: image-related freelancers (design, image editing, art): jobs -3.7% and earnings -9.4% after DALL-E 2 and Midjourney. Confirmed in Table A3 as log-like coefficients. The \"after\" period is coded from April 2022.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U41: the authors call the skill-level results suggestive. The paper adds a caveat the dossier lacks: it cannot see off-platform work, so a fall on Upwork may be a move elsewhere rather than lost work.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U103: the published abstract says past performance does not protect freelancers, top freelancers may be hit disproportionately (suggestive), and AI may reduce overall demand for workers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U14/F4: Demirci, Hannane and Zhu (Management Science). Job posts for automation-prone work fell 21% against manual work within eight months of ChatGPT; image-creation posts fell 17%; the remaining jobs were more complex and paid more. Confirmed from the authors' abstract.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a20"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U24/U39: the Demirci details (writing about 30%, 1.4 million posts, July 2021 to July 2023) come from press write-ups only, and they disagree on sample size: one says 1.4 million listings, another \"almost two million\" across 61 countries. The full paper was not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-a21"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U16/F4 attribute arXiv 2308.05201 (\"treated submarkets lost about 22%, bids fell 16.8%, cannot tell replaced from never posted\") to Demirci, Hannane and Zhu. WRONG PAPER: arXiv 2308.05201 is Liu, Nan, Li, Tan and co-authors, a different study of a different (undisclosed) platform, Sept 2021 to Aug 2023. Its own figures: postings -22.1%, bids -16.8%. Its usage caveat is confirmed. On coding: the competition increase was especially pronounced in programming submarkets, so \"coding was not hit more\" misreads it.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a22"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F4 presents image freelancing as clearly hit (Hui -3.7% jobs; Demirci -17% posts). The Liu et al. paper tested text-to-image AI on image-related submarkets and found NO significant effect. The image result is therefore contested across studies, not settled.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a23"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U83: \"two studies of the same platform\". Not shown. Hui names Upwork. Demirci says only \"a leading global freelancing platform\" and Liu only \"a leading freelancer platform\". Do not say \"same platform\" and do not name one for Demirci.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a24"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U72/F4: Teutloff et al. (Journal of Economic Behavior and Organization, 2025). 116 skill clusters; substitutable skills such as writing and translation down 20-50% against trend, sharpest for 1-3 week jobs; complementary skills up; novice workers down. The \"3M+ postings\" size is not on the saved page and was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a25"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U62/F5: GDC 2026 (2,300+ respondents). 28% laid off in the past two years, 33% in the US, half say their employer had layoffs in the past 12 months. 36% use generative AI (30% at studios, 58% in publishing, support and marketing).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a26"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U53/F5: 52% say generative AI is having a negative impact (30% in 2025, 18% the year before); 7% positive (down from 13%); visual and technical artists most negative at 64%; students cite AI-led displacement among their worries.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a27"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F5: GDC 2025: 11% laid off in the past year; reasons restructuring 22%, revenue 18%, market shifts 15%, no reason 19%. AI is not among the reasons. The 19% refers to layoffs respondents observed. The survey had 3,000+ respondents.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a28"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F5: \"Generative AI use and hostility are both rising\". Hostility rose (A27), but personal use did NOT. 36% personally used it in GDC 2025 and 36% in GDC 2026. Company-level use was 52% in 2025.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-a29"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U25: game layoff tallies (global). Game Industry Layoff Tracker: 10,500 (2023), 14,600 (2024), 5,300 (2025). Satvat: 15,631 (2024), 9,053 (2025). Satvat's 2022-23 figures reuse the tracker's, and the tracker's own 2025 rows sum to 5,931. Secondary source (GamesBeat).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a30"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F5: \"Layoffs peaked in 2024, before most studios shipped AI assets\". The 2024 peak is confirmed (A30). The \"before most studios shipped AI assets\" half has no source in the dossier or in anything opened here.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-a31"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U42: a Hangzhou recruiter (Leo Li) told Rest of World (Viola Zhou, 11 April 2023) that illustrator jobs in China's game industry fell about 70% in a year. He blamed regulation, the economy AND AI. China only, one recruiter's estimate.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a32"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U86: a Chongqing studio laid off 5 of 15 character-design illustrators. The source is an illustrator at the studio, who thinks AI was \"partly to blame\". A freelancer (Amber Yu, 3,000-7,000 yuan per poster) is now offered a tenth of her rate for fixes. NetEase says the aim is better tools. Tencent and miHoYo did not respond.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a33"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U69/F14: Danish linked employer-employee study (Humlum and Vestergaard). Precise nulls on earnings and hours, ruling out effects above 2% two years after ChatGPT; new AI tasks are widespread. The paper has since been retitled (\"Still Waters, Rapid Currents\"). The \"11 occupations\" count is not in the current abstract and was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a34"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Critique search for the Danish study: the main counterpoint is Brynjolfsson, Chandar and Chen (\"Canaries\"). US payroll data show entry-level hiring in AI-exposed jobs down 13% within firms, relative to less-exposed jobs. The Danish authors' later version finds no entry-level gap. Stanford's summary: overall hiring has not declined meaningfully because of AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a35"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U97/F14: Autor, Chin, Salomons and Seegmiller. Most current US jobs are in specialties introduced after 1940; automating innovations slow new work; the demand-eroding effect of automation has intensified, while the demand-raising effect of augmentation has not. Published in the QJE in 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a36"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U15/F14: Eloundou et al. measure exposure without separating augmentation from displacement, and flag a phase of writers becoming freelancers. Creative writing is among the most exposed groups (\"Poets, Lyricists and Creative Writers\"). Graphic designers appear only in a highest-disagreement list. No musician, actor, photographer or voice-actor rows were found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-a37"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F6 (voice-acting finding). The dossier says NAVA's 21% \"lost work directly to AI\" and that \"no official labour data covers voice actors\". Checked: the NAVA survey question behind the 21% asks whether the respondent has EVER knowingly lost A job to a synthetic voice. It is a cumulative, at-least-one-gig measure from a self-selected sample of an advocacy group. It is not the share who lost work this year, and it says nothing about how much work or income was lost. BLS does cover voice work, but folds it into Actors with no separate count (see B27). The APA, ElevenLabs and $2,100-average parts hold (B5, B15).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U46. NAVA's own May 2026 press release (advocacy) does say about 21% of voice actors lost work directly to AI, up from 14% a year earlier. That wording overstates what the question asked: the saved chart (2026 part 3, chart 8) reads Yes 21% / No 79% to the question in B1, a lifetime knowingly-lost-a-job question. Say \"about one in five respondents to a voluntary survey by a voice actors' advocacy group say they have knowingly lost at least one job to an AI voice\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U46 baseline. The 2025 NAVA survey asked the same lifetime question; the saved 2025 chart (part 3, chart 7) reads Yes 14% / No 86%. The chart title omits the word ever but the page text includes it. 2025: 1,305 responses, open 5 to 21 Feb 2025. So 14% to 21% is a like-for-like rise in the same advocacy convenience sample frame, not a measured rate of job loss.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U47. NAVA 2026 method: 1,379 responses, open 22 Jan to 22 Feb 2026, recruited from unsponsored social posts, voiceover Facebook groups and NAVA's contact list of about 4,000; no identifying information collected (so duplicates cannot be checked). No weighting or verification is described anywhere on the page. Advocacy source.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U2. Audio Publishers Association (trade association) via Library Journal infoDOCKET: 2025 US audiobook sales $2.43B (+9%); AI-narrated titles and their consumption grew in 2025; willingness to try AI narration fell from 70% (2025 survey) to 61% (2026 survey); 16% of audiobook listeners have heard an AI-voiced audiobook; AI sales revenue .03% of 2025. Caveat, not verified: the sales figure comes from a survey of reporting publishers (Toluna), and nothing in the release says whether self-published AI titles (KDP Virtual Voice, Apple digital narration) are inside it, so .03% may undercount AI narration.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U34. TechCrunch (May 2025) confirms 50,000+ titles labelled Narrated By: Virtual Voice on Audible, a search-label count. The 40,000 Bloomberg/Publishers Lunch figure (May 2024) was not opened. The 750,000+ active titles is the APA's count of titles reported by publishers (B5), a different denominator from the Audible catalogue, so the two cannot be divided into a share. The 50,000 is now 17 months old.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U77. Audible's May 2025 publisher programme: select publishers, 100+ AI voices in English, French, Spanish and Italian, AI translation beta into Spanish, French, Italian and German: confirmed. The Audible-managed vs self-service and exclusive vs non-exclusive details are not in the saved TechCrunch text and were not checked against an Audible primary.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U0. ElevenLabs API list price (vendor): $0.08 per 1K characters on v4, v3 and v2 Multilingual; $0.04 on v4 Turbo, v3 Conversational and Flash/Turbo. The page itself pairs 10,000 characters with about 10 minutes and $0.08 per 1K with about $0.08 per minute, so about $4.80 per finished hour (and $2.40 on the cheaper models) is a fair derivation, ours not theirs. Caution: ElevenLabs' own payouts blog equates 1,000 characters to roughly 90 seconds, which would make the hour cheaper still.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U59. Wrong headline: subscription is NOT cheaper than API list price. The API-plan table charges the same $0.08 per minute at every tier (Creator 275,000 characters for $22, Pro 1,238,000 for $99), i.e. $4.80/hour, identical to list. The consumer app (UI) plans are dearer: Creator $22 for 121,000 credits, about 121 minutes (about $0.18 per minute, roughly $11/hour); Pro $99 for about 600 minutes. So a 20-minute narration costs about $1.60 through the API or about $3.60 at the app's Creator rate, before re-takes. Both prices are vendor list prices that change often.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U3. The ACX figures are real but the post is from June 2013, 13 years old, and must not be presented as today's rate. It gives roughly $200 PFH for narration plus $200 PFH for post-production, about four hours of work per finished hour, and about 9,300 words per finished hour. It does not say $300-$400 PFH is common; it says that rate may seem high. Royalty share (50-50 split of the rights holder's royalty, no upfront fee) only with ACX exclusive distribution, exclusive royalty escalating from 50%: confirmed as of 2013; current ACX royalty terms were not checked.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U4 (Apple). Apple Books digital narration is free to produce, delivered via Apple's preferred partners, wholesale pricing with tier limits, publisher or author keeps audiobook rights with no restriction on other versions. Vendor page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U4 (Amazon royalty). KDP audiobooks with virtual voice: list price $3.99 to $14.99, 40% royalty on all sales during the beta, with Amazon reserving a lower rate later for books sold below the minimum.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U4 (Amazon fee and scope). KDP virtual voice is a beta, free to create at this time, and only for books distributed in the US marketplace.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U10. ElevenLabs (vendor self-report, published 25 Jun 2025): voice actors earned a combined $5M through the Voice Library in under two years; default rate about $0.03 per 1,000 characters, up to $0.20 for HQ or rare voices; payout from a $10 balance. Two cautions: the same post later says over $1 million, so it is internally inconsistent, and it says contributors need not be professional voice actors, so voice actors here means anyone who uploaded a voice. No longer the largest figure (B15).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U19. ElevenLabs (vendor self-report, 22 May 2026): $11M by November 2025, over $22M six months later; 10,400+ creators earning; earnings from usage on terms the creator sets. The $2,100 average is our division ($22M / 10,400), and it mixes a cumulative total (payouts began Feb 2024, B16) with a present-tense headcount; the distribution is undisclosed and the creators include non-professionals.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U65. ElevenLabs (vendor, 28 Oct 2024): payouts began in February 2024; over $1M earned by 28 Oct 2024. No headcount, no distribution.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U66. ElevenLabs Iconic Marketplace (vendor, 11 Nov 2025): Michael Caine plus over 25 other voices, companies request access; the pool includes estates of dead figures (Maya Angelou, Alan Turing), via agencies such as CMG Worldwide. No payment terms, earnings or deal counts in the announcement. The roughly 28 total from press was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U37. SAG-AFTRA and Replica Studios (Jan 2024), per law firm Loeb: members could license digital voice replicas for R&D and scripted video-game content; pay based on the Interactive Media Agreement; consent per new project; time limits before further payment and consent. NOT verified from a primary: that SAG-AFTRA flagged the contract no longer in effect in September 2025, and the session-fee minimum (sagaftra.org blocked; Wayback rate-limited). No figure for actors signed or money paid was found.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U82 (deal terms). SAG-AFTRA and Narrativ (Aug 2024), per Variety: members negotiate a fee per project, not below the union's commercials-contract minimum; consent for every use; a pension and health contribution per ad. That makes it per-use licensing, not a royalty share. The dossier's addendum-to-the-Audio-Commercials-contract wording is not in the saved text.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U82 (follow-up). SiriusXM Media press release, 17 Jun 2025 (company source): voice actors who opt in to Narrativ can earn additional income and approve or reject every ad. No rates, no number of actors, no money paid.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b20"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U33. Non-union rates from the GVAA rate guide as reproduced by Voicecrafters, a voice-over firm (vendor, page titled 2026), for online non-broadcast narration: 1-2 min $350-$450 through 40-60 min $1,750-$2,350, ranges per job by finished length; 15-20 min $800-$950, 20-30 min $950-$1,250. So a 15-25 minute narration brackets roughly $800-$1,250 per job. These are asking-rate guides, not what was actually paid.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b21"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U26. Society of Authors (UK writers' union) survey, January 2024, 787 responses: 26% of illustrators and 36% of translators say they already lost work to generative AI; 37% and 43% say their income lost value. UK, self-selected members' survey, not voice.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b22"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U73. Association of Illustrators (UK, advocacy) early-2025 survey, per Design Week: over 32% of respondents lost work to AI, average £9,262 per affected artist. The 6,844 and 32.4% figures and the AOI primary were not checked. UK, not voice.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b23"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U43. Japan's NOMORE campaign: 26 voice actors, launched 21 Oct 2024, preview video 15 Oct with 87,000+ views, backed by the Japan Audio Producers' Association and the Japan Actors Union, targeting AI voice models trained without permission. The group says it does not oppose generative AI itself: not found in the saved article.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-b24"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U87 (database). Japan's largest actors and voice-artists union with Itochu and its systems arm launched what they called the country's first official voice database against AI misuse (MLex, 17 Nov 2025; paywalled teaser only).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b25"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U87 (guidelines). The consent guidelines are a separate, later event, not part of the Nov 2025 launch: the Japan Actors Union announced voluntary generative-AI voice guidelines on 15-16 October 2026, calling for consent at every stage (training, generation, publication).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-b26"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"GAP: official labour data. BLS Occupational Outlook Handbook: voice-over and narration work is inside the Actors occupation, with no separate voice count. Actors: 67,500 jobs in 2025, projected little or no change 2025-35 (about 6,300 openings a year). That is a projection, not a measurement of AI's effect, and BLS wage-and-salary counts miss much freelance voice work.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b27"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"GAP: what synthetic voices pay working voice actors, from NAVA's own survey (advocacy). Among respondents who answered how much their synthetic voice earned in 2025, the largest single group, 88 people, reported $0 (2026 part 3, chart 3, read from the saved image); the non-zero answers are scattered thinly from $15 to $120,000.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b28"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"GAP: the vendor's own cost claim for a whole audiobook. ElevenLabs (vendor) says independent authors make full-length audiobooks with marketplace voices for under $100 in credits, and quotes a creator for whom human production was out of reach at $3,000+ per title. Vendor marketing, not a measured average.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b29"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"GAP: Voices (a voice marketplace that sells actor-powered AI voices; vendor) Amplified 2026 report: a Censuswide survey of 700 business leaders and consumers; 79% of business leaders prioritize AI voices from real, attributed actors. Buyer sentiment, no count of jobs, bookings or income.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b30"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"GAP: Voices 2025 Audio Trends Report (vendor, Dec 2024): a survey of people who hire voice actors; strong stated preference for human voices; nearly 80% of AI-voice buyers put human-likeness first. Sentiment, not volume.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b31"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"GAP: peer-reviewed work. The only peer-reviewed voice-specific study found is a FAccT 2025 paper built on interviews with 15 professional voice artists: qualitative, no earnings or job counts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-b32"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"GAP: union earnings or session data. No public SAG-AFTRA figure for voice-over earnings, session counts or AI-replica payouts was found in eight searches; the SAG-AFTRA Replica FAQ and summary PDFs could not be opened (403, Wayback 429). Census nonemployer data were not pulled. Null as far as this pass reached.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-b33"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F9 / U95: under current US Copyright Office guidance, prompts alone do not make the user the author of AI output. The Office says this is based on current tools and could be revisited.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F9 / U95: purely AI-generated material is not protected. Human-authored expression in the output is protected, and so are creative selection, arrangement and modification of AI output. This is Office guidance, not statute.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U95: AI used as a tool still earns protection for the human part. The Office registered the Randy Travis track. Zarya of the Dawn's registration covered its text and arrangement but not its Midjourney images.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U95: the Office found no case for new sui generis rights in AI output.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U55 / F9: the D.C. Circuit (decided 2025-03-18) upheld the human-author requirement in Thaler v. Perlmutter. The opinion was opened: the dossier said it was not.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U55: rehearing was denied 2025-05-12, and the Supreme Court denied certiorari on 2026-03-02 (No. 25-449).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F10: Bartz v. Anthropic (N.D. Cal., Judge Alsup, order dated 2025-06-23). Training Claude on the books was fair use. Scanning purchased print books was fair use. Keeping pirated copies in a permanent central library was not.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F10: the Bartz settlement is a $1.5B fund, about $3,000 per work before costs and fees, paid in four installments ending 2027-09-27. It releases past claims only, releases no claims over AI outputs, is not a licence to train, and requires destruction of the LibGen and PiLiMi copies.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Bartz status as of 2026-10-27: final approval was granted 2026-07-20. The parties say the Effective Date has passed. The first payments are expected on or before 2026-11-15, so none had gone out by the date of this check. Whether the installments due at final approval and on 2026-09-25 were paid was not checked.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F10: Copyright Office Part 3 (pre-publication) says many training uses are likely transformative. It also says commercial use of vast troves of works to make competing expressive content, especially through illegal access, goes beyond established fair use.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U54: Part 3 calls government intervention premature, suggests extended collective licensing where markets fail, and is still a pre-publication version. The Office's AI page says no substantive changes are expected.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Same claim (U54), the Office's AI page: the final version of Part 3 is still to come.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F10 scriptTrap said the \"market dilution\" passage in Part 3 only summarizes commenters and must not be attributed to the Office. Wrong: after summarizing commenters, the Office adopts the dilution theory as its own view, with a caveat that this is uncharted territory. It can be attributed to the Office, with that caveat.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U54: the dossier says Register Perlmutter \"was dismissed\" and her suit is unresolved. The suit is still unresolved, but she is back in office under a D.C. Circuit injunction. The Supreme Court denied the government's stay application on 2026-06-30, and the district court stayed the suit until 2026-12-18. Secondary source (IPWatchdog); the court orders were not opened.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"NEW, 2026 appellate ruling on training: Thomson Reuters v. ROSS (3d Cir., filed 2026-09-29). ROSS's training of a non-generative legal search tool on 2,243 Westlaw headnotes was not fair use. The court expressly set generative models (Bartz, OpenAI) apart. This is the first federal appellate ruling on AI training and fair use, and it is not about generative AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U49: Andersen v. Stability AI. The dossier said the hearing moved to 2027-02-17, plus \"a further three-month extension\". The 2026-09-28 order instead sets dispositive motions and class certification to be heard by 2027-09-29. There is still no merits ruling on fair use, and discovery fights with Midjourney and DeviantArt were ongoing in Oct 2026. The 2024-08-12 claim details were not re-checked against the order.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U49: what the blog reported, now superseded by C16.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U56: Getty v. Stability (N.D. Cal., No. 25-cv-06891-TLT). Getty dropped its Delaware suit on 2025-08-14 and refiled. On 2026-04-23 the court dismissed only Count II (DMCA 1202(a) false CMI), without prejudice and with leave to amend by 2026-05-07. The trademark, dilution and unfair-competition claims survived. The copyright count (Count I) was not part of the motion.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Getty status as of 2026-10-27: Stability answered (2026-05-21, amended 2026-07-02). The dispositive-motion hearing is set for 2027-08-31, so there is no fair-use ruling before late 2027.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U28 / F15: Lehrman v. Lovo (S.D.N.Y., Judge Oetken, 2025-07-10), now confirmed from the docket and the opinion. Survived: Sage's copyright claim over her actual recording, the contract claims, New York Civil Rights Law claims and NY GBL claims. Training-based copyright claims were dismissed with leave to amend. Everything else (copyright over the cloned voice, Lanham Act) was dismissed. A voice as such is not copyrightable.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c20"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U28: the court's own words on voices.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c21"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U28 / F15 status: the dossier's \"fact discovery to end May 5, 2026\" is stale. Discovery was extended to 2026-08-21. Lovo then filed a suggestion of bankruptcy on 2026-05-27, and the case has been STAYED since 2026-05-28. A motion to dismiss the second amended complaint's sixth cause of action, set for argument on 2026-06-16, is stayed with it. A press snippet says Chapter 7; the bankruptcy filing itself was not opened.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c22"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U28: the second amended complaint was filed 2025-07-31.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c23"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U93: the WGA 2023 MBA (union summary, primary). AI can't write literary material, and AI material is not source material. A writer may use AI with company consent, but the company can't require it. AI material handed to a writer must be disclosed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c24"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U94: the WGA Know Your Rights page (primary). Neither traditional nor generative AI is a writer. Unpublished generative-AI material is not assigned or source material. The WGA reserves the training argument.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c25"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"NEW status (U93/U94/F15): the 2023 MBA has been succeeded by the 2026 MBA, ratified 90.38% for a four-year term. It keeps the 2023 AI terms and adds written notice to the Guild when studios license writers' work to train generative AI. Trade press only (Screen Daily). The Guild's own 2026 summary was not found.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c26"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U27: the 2025 SAG-AFTRA Interactive Media Agreement requires informed consent for each AI use and lets performers suspend consent during a strike. \"Real-time generation\" pays at least 7.5x scale, and AI was the sole impetus of the 320-day strike. Secondary (TheWrap). The \"sole impetus\" line is TheWrap's wording; that it came from a SAG-AFTRA release was not verified.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c27"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U75: IMA consent must be in a separate document or signed rider, with a reasonably specific description that includes real-time generation. For most uses it can't be taken at initial hiring for future games. There is a usage report within 90 days of release, and \"traditional AI\" and processing practices are excluded. Secondary (law-firm summary).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c28"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U98: SAG-AFTRA TV/Theatrical 2023. Confirmed in the Loeb summary: signed or initialed consent for employment-based replicas; consent, bargaining and pension and health contributions for independently created replicas; notice and bargaining for synthetic performers; consent where a principal facial feature is recognizable. NOT in the summary, which excludes pay terms: \"reasonably specific description\", \"paid as time otherwise worked\" and residuals. The SAG-AFTRA primary was blocked.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c29"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"NEW status (U98/F15): the 2023 TV/Theatrical contract has been replaced by the 2026 TV/Theatrical Agreement. It was ratified 91.4% and runs July 1, 2026 to June 30, 2030, with expanded AI and digital-replica terms that further restrict synthetic performers. The new terms are from trade press only, and their exact text was not seen.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c30"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Same 2026 agreement (U98/F15), second trade report: what the new terms restrict.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c31"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U31 / F15: California AB 2602 (Labor Code 927). Mostly right, but the dossier leaves out an exception. A vague replica clause is still enforceable if the uses are consistent with the contract and with the fundamental character of the performance as recorded. Applies only to new performances fixed on or after 2025-01-01. A lawyer-negotiated deal or a union CBA that expressly covers replicas defeats it. Statute text is via california.public.law (a reproduction).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c32"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U76: California AB 1836 (Civil Code 3344.1) on deceased performers. Consent is needed for commercial replicas in expressive audiovisual works and sound recordings, with exceptions for news, comment, criticism, satire, parody, fleeting use and documentary use. Damages are at least $750. Law-firm summary only; the statute was not opened.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c33"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U29: Tennessee ELVIS Act (Public Chapter 588). Voice, including simulations, is added. There is civil liability for knowingly making an unauthorized voice or likeness public, and for distributing a tool whose primary purpose is producing a specific person's voice or likeness.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c34"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U29: the ELVIS Act took effect 2024-07-01, carries Class A misdemeanor exposure and is limited to Tennessee residents. Secondary (Holland & Knight). The OCR of the Act did not yield the effective-date section.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c35"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U30: the Cash Trust v. Coca-Cola ELVIS Act suit (federal court, Nashville; reported 2025-11-27) is over a human sound-alike in an ad that began airing in August 2025, and seeks damages and an injunction. The docket number and the case status since Nov 2025 were NOT checked, so \"no ruling\" is stale-risk.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c36"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U32 / F15 status: the dossier says the NO FAKES Act still needs committee and floor votes. Senate Judiciary has now ordered S. 4591 reported (2026-06-18) with a substitute amendment, reported it 2026-06-24, and it is on the Senate calendar (No. 446). It is still a bill. No floor vote appears in the API action list. H.R. 8915 sits in House Judiciary.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c37"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U32: the House bill's last action is referral to committee (2026-05-20).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c38"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U32: the 2025 text (S. 1367). Damages are the greater of actual damages or $5,000 per work for an individual. Online services that made a good-faith compliance effort and non-service entities pay $25,000 per work. Services without good faith pay $5,000 per display or copy, up to $750,000 per work. The dossier's \"$25,000 for others\" is a simplification. The post-mortem right runs 10 years, is renewable in 5-year steps and ends at 70 years at most. It preempts state replica claims but saves state laws in effect on 2025-01-02. The reported S. 4591 keeps the same damage and preemption structure.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c39"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U17: Japan's Copyright Act Art. 30-4 allows uses where enjoying the work's expression is not the purpose, including data analysis, with a proviso for uses that unreasonably prejudice the owner.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c40"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U17: the Agency for Cultural Affairs \"General Understanding\". The exception does not apply when an enjoyment purpose coexists, for example additional training on a small group of one creator's works in order to output their common expression. The saved file is the May 2024 English OVERVIEW; the 15 March 2024 date of the main document was not seen. The text says \"a small group of works\", not \"even a small amount of training data\". It is non-binding.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c41"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U18: the UK s.136 report (March 2026). It received 11,520 responses, 3% backed the opt-out exception, and the exception is no longer the preferred way forward. It makes no new law, and it will explore a digital replica or personality right. The document is dated \"March 2026\"; the day (18 March) comes from secondaries.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c42"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U18: a summer 2026 UK consultation on digital replicas (Reed Smith). No primary was found, and a search returned only law-firm commentary.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c43"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U100: EU AI Act Art. 50(2). Providers of generators must mark output machine-readably, with an exemption for assistive editing. The duty is on the provider. The text is from artificialintelligenceact.eu, a secondary reproduction of the Act. It does not reflect any Omnibus amendment to Art. 50 timing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c44"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U101: Art. 50(4) deep-fake disclosure by deployers. It is light-touch for evidently artistic or fictional works. The dossier's inference goes too far: Art. 50(4) applies only to a \"deep fake\", meaning content resembling existing persons, places or events that would falsely appear authentic. An invented AI-voiced character may not be a deep fake at all. It is also EU law, not US law.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c45"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U44: the Digital Omnibus on AI was published in the OJ on 2026-07-24 and in force 2026-07-27. The marking duty has a grace period to 2026-12-02 only for systems already on the market before 2026-08-02. Secondary (Lewis Silkin).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c46"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U44: the final Code of Practice on marking and labelling was published 2026-06-10. Secondary (Jones Day); the Commission page was not fetched.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c47"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U106: AI Act Art. 53(1)(c)-(d). A GPAI provider must have a copyright policy that honours TDM opt-outs and must publish a training-content summary. Secondary reproduction of the Act.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c48"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U106: the GPAI Code was published 2025-07-10 (Commission page via Wayback).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-c49"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U106, the GPAI Code copyright chapter: signatories \"commit\" to follow robots.txt and other appropriate machine-readable opt-out protocols. The dossier's \"best efforts\" wording does not appear in the measure. The \"piracy domains\" exclusion covers sites recognised by EU/EEA courts or authorities as persistent commercial-scale infringers, not piracy in general. The code is voluntary.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c50"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F10 as a whole. Correct on Bartz and Part 3, but it misdescribes the Part 3 dilution passage (see C13). It says the Andersen and Getty cases are \"still open\", which is true, but the dates have moved (C16, C19). It also misses the first appellate ruling (C15).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-c51"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F15 as a whole. The \"7.5x for real-time generation\" term is the 2025 video-game (IMA) contract only, not TV/Theatrical. Both the WGA and SAG-AFTRA TV/Theatrical contracts were renegotiated in 2026 (C26, C30). NO FAKES has cleared Senate committee (C37). Lovo is stayed by bankruptcy (C22). The dossier sentence needs those four updates.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c52"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F9 scriptTrap said \"the Thaler opinion itself was not opened\". It has now been opened and confirms the holding (C5). Nothing else in F9 changes.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-c53"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Concord v. Anthropic (music publishers) and In re OpenAI (S.D.N.Y.) are pending training cases that could produce rulings before the episode airs. Neither was checked in this pass beyond the ROSS footnote, which mentions a DOJ statement of interest in In re OpenAI (2026-09-01).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-c54"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U63: in the customer-support field rollout, productivity rose 14% on average, 34% for novices, with minimal effect on experts (NBER w31161, April 2023).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U8: Noy and Zhang, 444 professionals: time fell 0.8 SD, quality rose 0.4 SD (working-paper version read).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U8: the gain compressed the gap between workers and mostly substituted for effort.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U7: Doshi and Hauser (293 writers, 600 evaluators): AI story ideas raised creativity most for the less creative writers (+10.7% novelty for low-DAT writers with five ideas; +8.1% for all writers with five ideas). Note: writers were UK-based Prolific participants, not professional writers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U7: the AI-assisted stories became more similar to each other (homogenization).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U7 sample: the writers were not professionals and were recruited in the UK (matters for a US audience).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U5: on one art-sharing platform (4M+ artworks, 50,000+ users), text-to-image AI raised adopters' productivity about 25% and favorites per view about 50%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U5: favorites became less concentrated among adopters, but average novelty fell. These are existing artists who adopted AI, not newcomers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U64: Dell'Acqua et al. (758 BCG consultants): inside the frontier, 12.2% more tasks, 25.1% faster; outside it, 19% less likely to be correct. The paper's own conclusion calls the same drop 19 percentage points. The PDF read is a copy of the INFORMS article hosted on a third-party site.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U64: the lower-skilled consultants gained most; the authors use the word \"equalizer\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U9: Eisenmann et al. (50 professional artists vs matched laypeople): expertise still helped with a text-to-image tool.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F7/U9: Hou et al. (Information Systems Research): in implementation, AI kept helping low-expertise designers but not experts. The paper itself was not retrievable; the direction is confirmed from the university's own release, the \"lost efficiency\" magnitude is not.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U48: Adobe (vendor) job postings: US creative-professional postings rose 8% in six months (Sept 2025 to Apr 2026); the share naming AI skills rose from 10% to 15%. The base is small: about 10,500 to 11,300 postings.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U48 context: the same Adobe report says people training to enter creative work see AI as their way in, and that some clients now make low-stakes work themselves. Survey and interviews, not a measurement of new entrants' earnings.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U89: Goldman Sachs projected the creator economy roughly doubling from $250B to $480B by 2027, 50 million creators growing 10-20% a year, about 4% professionals over $100,000. The dossier dated it June 2023 and cited a Substack; the primary is Goldman's own page, dated 19 April 2023. It is a projection, not a measurement, and names cheaper creation technology as one driver, not AI.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U97: Autor, Chin, Salomons and Seegmiller: most current US employment is in job specialties introduced after 1940; automation's demand-eroding effect has intensified while augmentation's demand-raising effect has not.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F16/U104: on 15 July 2025 YouTube renamed \"repetitious\" to \"inauthentic content\", which was already ineligible to monetize; AI content from generic templates is listed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F16/U104: YouTube says using AI tools in a well-researched, original video stays allowed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F16/U88: the \"AI personas on sensitive topics\" rule. The dossier had it only from TechCrunch as \"health and finance\". YouTube's own page now carries it, and the list is wider: health, legal issues, finances, or politics, for content presented as a human expert giving advice.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F16/U88: TechCrunch reports the clarification rolled out on 16 July (2026) to all Partner Program members, in three categories; it describes AI personas as representations of real people made with AI. No enforcement counts.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-20"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F16/U105: realistic AI-generated or altered content must be disclosed; non-realistic content and minor edits are exempt.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-21"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U105: repeated non-disclosure can bring a label applied by YouTube, removal, or suspension from the Partner Program.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-22"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U1: OpenAI images cost cents each at list price. The page has moved on since the finder read it: it now leads with GPT Image 2.5, priced at $30 per million image output tokens, with an example of about $0.006 per image. The $0.053 and $0.211 per-image figures are not on today's page.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-23"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U59: ElevenLabs API text-to-speech lists $0.08 per 1,000 characters (about $0.08 a minute, so about $4.80 an hour, our arithmetic), and the Creator plan is $22 a month with 275k characters on the API page.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-24"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U59: the \"30k credits\" figure the finder tied to Creator belongs to the Starter plan. In the app, Creator is $22 ($11 the first month) with 121,000 credits, about 121 minutes: about $10.90 an hour at plan price (our arithmetic).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d1-25"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U60: Runway Gen-4.5 video. Max ($76 a month billed yearly, 9,500 credits = 791 s) works out to about $5.76 per generated minute; Standard (625 credits = 52 s at $12 yearly or $15 monthly) to about $13.85-$17.31 (our arithmetic). The dossier's \"$6 to $14\" leaves out monthly billing.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-26"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U61: Claude Pro is $20 a month ($17 a month billed yearly at $200), Max is from $100 with 5x or 20x Pro's usage, Team seats are $25 ($20 yearly), and Claude Code comes with every paid plan.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-27"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F11: SAG-AFTRA Corporate/Educational & Non-Broadcast 2025-28: off-camera voice-over first hour $550 (Cat I) or $614 (Cat II), $161 per extra half-hour; health and retirement 20.25% on top. The copy read is hosted by a third party; SAG-AFTRA's own page would not load. Whether a public YouTube channel fits this contract is not established by the sheet.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-28"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F11: EFA 2026 median ranges: fact-checking $50-60 an hour, research $47.50-60 an hour. The $60-72.50 figure is fact-checking per page, not per hour. Survey of 1,100+ EFA members on 2025 work, mostly book and editorial work.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-29"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U57: EFA \"Other Writing\", which covers screenplays and scripts, has a median range of $70-100 an hour; ghostwritten blog posts are $75-100 and business/marketing $87.50-125 an hour.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-30"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U58: BLS: film and video editors' median wage was $75,420 (May 2025), about $36.26 an hour at 2,080 hours (our arithmetic); 35% are self-employed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-31"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U58: BLS: special effects artists and animators, median $102,030 a year, $49.06 an hour (May 2025); 63% self-employed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-32"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (writer): BLS writers and authors, median $76,910 a year, $36.98 an hour (May 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-33"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (researcher/fact-checker, employee benchmark): BLS editors, median $77,920 a year, $37.46 an hour (May 2025). BLS has no fact-checker occupation.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-34"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (designer): BLS graphic designers, median $62,960 a year, $30.27 an hour (May 2025).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-35"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (sound mixer): BLS sound engineering technicians, also called sound mixers, median $73,130 a year (May 2025), about $35.16 an hour (our arithmetic). No US freelance mixing rate was found in this pass.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-36"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (actors, all kinds, employee wage): BLS median hourly wage for actors, $29.05 (May 2025). Shown for scale only; voice work is priced by session, not by this wage.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-37"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (narrator, non-union): the GVAA guide, as reproduced by a VO firm (vendor; GVAA's own guide is behind a sign-up form), prices online explainer narration of 10-15 finished minutes at $700-$800, 15-20 at $800-$950 and 20-30 at $950-$1,250.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-38"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (character voices, non-union): same reproduction: a basic session fee of $250-$350 an hour.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-39"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (voice, marketplace, vendor): Upwork says voice actors on its platform cost $40-$85 an hour, and video editors typically $6-$25 an hour; Upwork says these are worldwide historical contracts, not US rates. Its top video-editor band ($61-150) has a median of $100 an hour.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-40"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (motion designer/animator, vendor rate guide): School of Motion's freelance day rates run from $350 a day for newcomers to $800-1,000 for those who can run their own studio. Its 2026 guide (compiled from salary sites and its community) gives $20-$40 an hour for beginners and $80-$150 for senior designers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-41"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (motion designer, tiers): same publisher's 2026 table.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d1-42"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Team cost (thumbnail, vendor blog): a thumbnail costs $5-$55 on Fiverr (Fiverr's average job is $18) and $25-$100 from an Upwork freelancer; custom top end $300-$500 an episode. Upwork's illustrators run $15-$30 an hour. The thumbnail page is a vendor blog dated 2026-09-25.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d1-43"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Spotify (company self-report, published 2026-10-20, 'One Year In: Putting Human Artistry First on Spotify'): more than 50M AI slop and spam tracks taken out of recommendations in 2026. That means demoted, not deleted.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Spotify: 75M+ spammy tracks removed altogether. This is a separate bucket from P1, so never add the two into '125M deleted'. ⚠ The same '75 million' figure was given in Sept 2025 for the previous 12 months (D2-7), so it reads as a running total that has not been updated, not 75M more this year.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Spotify labels thousands of artist identities it believes are AI-generated as 'AI Persona', and keeps them out of editorial and algorithmic recommendations by default. The label is not applied to every track that used AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Spotify: more than 300,000 verified artists, over 70% of them independent","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Spotify: AI Credits launched in April 2026; artists file tens of thousands a day saying which roles and instruments used AI. This is the 'AI as a declared instrument' side.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Spotify: tracks that are likely AI-made are 10 times more likely to fall below the 1,000-stream royalty threshold, which has been in place since 2024","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Spotify admits its AI detection has limits. Say so whenever its figures are used.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Deezer (company self-report), Paris, 2026-10-13: an on/off toggle that removes fully AI-generated music from search, coming globally before the end of 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Deezer: nearly 90,000 fully AI tracks a day, more than 50% of daily delivery on peak days. This is a share of uploads, not of listening.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Deezer: more than 42M AI tracks kept out of recommendations, January 2025 to August 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Deezer: more than 14M albums tagged since it began labelling in June 2025","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Deezer/Ipsos survey (commissioned by the company, Nov 2025, 9,000 people in 8 countries): 45% want to filter out fully AI music. In the same survey, 97% could not tell AI music from human music in a blind test.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Deezer/Ipsos blind test: 97% could not tell fully AI music from human-made music (two AI songs and one real song). This is audience evidence for the brief.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"SDNY release, 2026-10-06: a North Carolina man got 18 months in prison, two years of supervised release and a forfeiture of $8,091,843.64","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"He pleaded guilty on 2026-03-19 to conspiracy to commit wire fraud, before Judge Koeltl, who passed the sentence","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Up to 10,000 bot accounts at once; hundreds of thousands of AI-made songs streamed billions of times; more than $8M in royalties. The scheme ran 2017 to 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The release uses the EO 14434 wording 'super intelligence'. Quote it as written, or say 'AI' in our own voice.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"'First-ever federal criminal streaming-fraud case' appears in press coverage only (WUNC/AP). The DOJ release does not say it. Keep it off air.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-p18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Federal Register 2026-20537, published Wednesday 2026-10-07: Copyright Office notice of inquiry, 'Music Streaming Fraud', Docket 2026-6; comments due 2026-11-23, replies due 2026-12-21","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Question 4 of the inquiry asks about the link between streaming fraud and AI-generated music","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p20"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Footnote 52 of the notice names the sentencing date, 10/6/2026 before Judge Koeltl (No. 24-cr-504)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-p21"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U13/U91 (F8). Deezer (self-report, 2026-07-21): fully AI tracks went past half of new uploads at the June 2026 peak, about 90,000 a day. ⚠ The release is internally inconsistent: one line says 'a monthly average of 90,000 tracks', others say 'nearly 90,000 … every day'. Say 'about 90,000 a day at peak'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U13: 'up from about 10% in January 2025'. The July release does not say this. It comes from Deezer's January 2025 release (the page has since been edited to add the 2026 figures).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The upload trajectory, from TechCrunch's summary of Deezer's earlier releases (secondary): 18% in April 2025, 28% in September 2025, 39% in January 2026, 44% in April 2026","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U13/U91: fully AI tracks are only 1 to 3% of streams on Deezer, and up to 85% of those streams were fraudulent in 2025 (self-report)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Context the dossier left out: Deezer itself says the hit to human artists' royalties on Deezer is 'minimal', because fraudulent streams are removed from the pool. Fraud across its whole catalogue was 8% of streams. This is a self-report and it cuts against 'the flood is draining artists' pay'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U91: Deezer will take down AI tracks used for streaming fraud and those not streamed for 6 months","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U68: Spotify (self-report, 2025-09-25) removed over 75M spammy tracks in the previous 12 months and warned that spam 'can dilute the royalty pool'. It gives no figure for royalties lost.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U68: the 'dilute' warning, worded as a possibility ('can'), not a measured loss","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U70 is the same claim as P1, P2 and P7 (Spotify 2026-10-20) and is checked there. 50M were taken out of recommendations; 75M were removed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U35 (F8): Chakrabarty, Liu, Ginsburg and Dhillon, arXiv 2607.20349, a working paper under review (v3, 2026-08-03). 14,419 self-published genre-fiction ebooks; the number of books with sales in a quarter grew 19.2-fold while quarterly revenue grew 8.9-fold. ⚠ The sample is books released from January 2023, so part of the fold growth comes from the sample filling up from a small 2023 base. Say 'revenue per selling book fell' (D2-12), not '19 times more books'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U35: books with more than 25% detected AI text are 20% of the sample, 12.1% of sales and 11.3% of revenue. Their share of sales rose to about 20% by 2026 Q2.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U35: comparing launch windows of equal length, revenue per book that sold at least one copy fell from the 2023 to the 2025 cohort, in six of eight genres overall and seven of eight for books with no AI text. The authors say this is not just a mix effect. It is the strongest evidence that the flood dilutes income for human authors too.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U35 status: the arXiv page labels it a working paper under review, not peer-reviewed. The sales data come from a proprietary panel kept by a Big Five publisher.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U92 (F8): Amazon KDP lowered daily new-title limits to 3 a day in Sept 2023. The dossier says this happened 'amid AI-generated book floods', but Amazon said it had not seen a spike. The figure 3 came from press reports and was confirmed by KDP to the Authors Guild (advocacy source).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U92: the cap is 3 a day, and KDP said it may adjust it","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U79: Bowker via Publishers Weekly (secondary): US self-published print and e-book output rose 38.7% in 2025 to more than 3.5M, from 2.5M. Traditionally published titles rose 6.6% to 642,242. Bowker's analyst credits 'new tools' and does not say AI. 2025 was a rebound after self-publishing fell in 2024.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U79: the cause Bowker gives (tools, not specifically AI)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U50 (F8): Sulka Haro's census of 53,597 Steam games (blog, July 2023 to July 2026). AI-flagged launches rose from about 13 a month to about 530; about one in three new games carried the disclosure by mid-2026. The ~1% shown before 2024 is games that added the flag later, not early adoption.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U50: the same blog says non-AI launches grew only modestly while AI-flagged games made up 60 to 90% of the growth in monthly releases. On income, its review-count proxy puts AI's share of sales at about 10 to 27%, against about 33% of launches.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U51 (F8): How To Market A Game (blog, 2026-01-08): about 20,000 Steam releases in 2025 depending on the tracker (SteamDB 20,008; VGInsights 20,282; Gamalytic 20,353; GameDiscoverCo 20,558). Growth was 11%, the third-lowest rise since Steam Direct began.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-20"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U51: the author's own view is that the AI game flood 'hasn't come'. This is an opinion about calendar 2025. D2-19 covers to mid-2026 and finds AI-flagged games are most of the growth. The two are compatible: modest total growth with a rising AI share. Do not say Steam has seen an AI flood.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-21"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U40 (F8): Game Oracle (vendor analysis) via Notebookcheck: 9,879 Steam games released Jan to Oct 2025. Games disclosing AI averaged 4 reviews in their first month against 7 for the rest; ratings (games with 100+ reviews) 84.6% against 88.3%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-22"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U40: after controls, about 52.6% fewer reviews ('about half' in F8). Reviews stand in for sales.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-23"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U40 caveat, in the source itself: the gap cannot be blamed on AI. Budget, marketing and polish could explain it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-24"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U78: Totally Human's analysis via VGC (2025-07-17, secondary). The article says 'nearly 8,000', not '7,818'. It compares this with '1,000 a year ago', a running total a year earlier, not '1,000 released during 2024'. The shares are 7% of the library and 20% of 2025 releases.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-25"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U96: Valve's Steam content-survey page (primary). The AI disclosure covers content that ships and that players consume; efficiency gains from AI tools are 'not the focus'. It has Pre-Generated and Live-Generated categories, and live AI needs stated guardrails.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-26"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U96: 'Valve rewrote the form in January 2026 to exempt internal development tools'. The saved page is undated and does not mention a rewrite. Unchecked.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-d2-27"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U99: Black Ops 7's Steam page discloses generative AI for some in-game assets without naming them; Activision's statement is generic (Insider Gaming, 2025-11-14, secondary)","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-28"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U99: the wording of the Steam disclosure as quoted","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-29"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U71: TikTok (company self-report, 2025-11-24) says its labelling has covered more than 1.3 billion videos. It requires labels on realistic AI content, reads C2PA Content Credentials, is adding invisible watermarks, and is testing an AI-content control in 'Manage topics'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-30"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U71: the For You control was announced as a test ('we'll start testing'), not a launched feature","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-31"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U20: Shutterstock Contributor Fund (company help page): a 20% average royalty rate on data-licence revenue, shared in proportion to how much of each contributor's content is in the datasets, paid 'periodically'. No total is disclosed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-32"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U20: paid when the fund 'accumulates significant revenue', with no fixed schedule","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-33"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U21: Adobe's third Firefly contributor bonus (Adobe staff post, 2025-09-17), based on content considered for training between 2024-06-03 and 2025-06-02 and the licences it earned. No pool total is given.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-34"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U21: 'bonuses under $1 are not paid' comes from an Adobe Community Expert reply, not Adobe staff","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-35"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U45: photographer Robert Kneschke's survey of 58 photographers, via TechCrunch 2023-09-30 (secondary): an average of $0.0078 a image and a median of $0.0069 from the Shutterstock fund, so about $15 for a 2,000-image portfolio","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-36"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U45: 'an estimated total of about $4.24M paid in the first round' is not in the TechCrunch article. Drop it. What the article does say is that no vendor would give a dollar figure.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-37"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U107: Getty (2023, via TechCrunch) said it would pay on an annual recurring, pro rata basis under an undisclosed 'set formula'","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-38"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U74: the CVL Economics study (AWN, 2024-02-12, secondary). 300 executives and managers surveyed 2023-11-17 to 12-22. It projects about 118,500 US film, TV and animation jobs (21.4%) with enough tasks affected by 2026. It was commissioned by four parties with a stake: Concept Art Association, The Animation Guild, Human Artistry Campaign and the National Cartoonist Society Foundation (the dossier named two). It is a forecast, not a count of jobs lost.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-39"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U74: who commissioned it","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-40"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U36 (F12): US photographers counted at 938 in 1850 and 20,040 in 1890. These figures come from a Cornell library exhibit timeline (secondary); the census tables themselves were not opened (a search found no primary table). On air, say 'counted by the census, as reported by Cornell's library', or round to 'under a thousand to about twenty thousand'.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-41"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U36: the 1890 figure","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-42"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U80 (F12): Edinburgh & Leith Post Office directories (a local-history site, secondary). Miniature painters 12 (1840), 9, 8, 3 (1870); photographers 0, 2, 27, 41. ⚠ These are businesses listed in a directory, not workers. In the same table, 'Artists' rose from 12 to 44 and portrait painters held at 26 to 24 until the 1880s. Only the miniaturists collapsed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-43"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U80: the same source says photographers created work for painters (retouching, portraits 'finished in oils'), so the trades overlapped as well as competed","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-44"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F12 typesetting (BLS Monthly Labor Review, May 1999, primary): hand compositors and linotype and monotype operators were last described in the 1986-87 Occupational Outlook Handbook","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-45"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F12: by the 1998-99 Handbook, customers were doing their own typesetting and layout ('desktop publishing'). This is the closest precedent for a customer doing the work themselves.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-46"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F12 nuance the dossier left out: BLS says new occupations replaced the old ones, including data-entry keyers and 'desktop-publishing specialists'. One trade ended and a smaller new one began.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-47"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U81 (F12): Deseret News/AP, 2004-01-13 (secondary): Disney closed its Orlando animation studio (about 258 jobs) while moving from hand-drawn to computer-made features. Animation staff went from a peak of 2,200 in 1999 to 600, all in Burbank. The driver the article names is box office (computer-animated films sold better) and a move to hiring per film. That is a change of medium, not a labour-saving tool.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-48"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U81: the stated cause","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-49"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U90 (F12): Petrillo said recording was ruining the jobs of 60% of AFM members. Source: the Local 802 union newspaper (advocacy), quoting Downbeat, 1942.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-50"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U90: the 8,000 to 9,000 unemployed figure and 'no firm statistics' are not in the Local 802 article cited. They appear in Allan Sutton's Mainspring Press history (a record-industry historian who is hostile to Petrillo; secondary). It describes a June 1941 AFM convention claim built on a survey with loaded questions.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-51"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U90 outcome. The dossier says it 'ended with a royalty fund for free concerts, not restored jobs'. The cited source says the fund paid wages to 45,000 musicians to play free live concerts, also paid out to unemployed members, and it calls the strike 'an overwhelming success'. Correct the framing: the fight bought a royalty-funded stream of live gigs, not the old jobs.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-52"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U90: how the 1942-44 ban ended, per Local 802 (advocacy): by November 1944 every label had agreed to pay royalties to the union","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-53"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U11 (F13): Horton et al., Scientific Reports 2023-11-03. Across six experiments (N = 2,965, including representative US samples), people valued art labelled AI-made lower, even when they said it was indistinguishable","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-54"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U11, the 62% mismatch, reconciled. The paper's 62% drop in monetary value is from its last experiment (Experiment 6), reported in a supplementary table that was not opened. The ~25% the dossier found is Experiment 4 (gallery price $148.35 for the human label against $111.74 for the AI label). Both come from the paper. On air, say 'valued it far lower'; if a figure is needed, use 'about a quarter lower' (Exp. 4, visible table).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-55"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U11: the value measures were hypothetical. Online panels paid $1 to $2 named a price for a gallery painting; no one bought anything.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-56"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U12 (F13): Abel and Johnson, IZA DP 17646 (January 2025, a discussion paper, provisional). 654 US Prolific participants, December 2024, in an incentivised design. The AI label lowered ratings but did not change reading time or willingness to pay or work, even for the 36% who said they prefer human writing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-57"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U85 (F13): Porter and Machery, Scientific Reports 2024. 1,634 non-expert participants scored 46.6% at telling ChatGPT-3.5 poems from real poets', below chance, and rated the AI poems higher on qualities such as rhythm and beauty","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-58"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U85 nuance the dossier left out: the same paper's second study found that being told a poem was human-written raised ratings on 12 of 14 qualities. Unlabelled AI does well, but the label bias is still there.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-59"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U84 (F13): Coca-Cola's AI holiday ad. The dossier says 2025, but it was the 2024 remake of 'Holidays Are Coming' (Adweek, 2024-11-20). F13 says it 'scored normally', but System1 gave it a perfect 5.9, the top of its scale, in both the US and UK. System1 is an ad-testing vendor; the quote is its executive's.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-60"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"U84: the backlash happened alongside the high panel score","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-d2-61"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F8, the headline sentence 'AI-disclosing games are now about a third of new releases, yet total releases grew only about 11% in 2025'. Both parts check out, but they cover different windows (calendar 2025 against mid-2026) and come from two different blogs. Do not put them in one sentence as if one dataset showed both.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-d2-62"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"F12, 'Petrillo … reportedly could not back with data': the claim stands only on the Mainspring Press account (D2-51), not on the source the dossier cites. Attribute it to that history, or leave it out.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-d2-63"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Pinterest (company newsroom, 2025-04-30) rolled out an \"AI modified\" label on image Pins it detects as AI-made or AI-edited, using the file's metadata plus its own detectors, and admits the detectors make mistakes (there is an appeal route).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The same post announced a \"see fewer\" test for AI Pins, starting with beauty and art.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"2025-10-16: Pinterest's \"tuner\" lets users dial down AI images by category (Android and desktop first); a 2025-12-12 update added food and drink.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Pinterest's own framing, \"Generative AI content … now making up an incredible 57% of all online material\", is a misread. Its footnote gives a paper title that doesn't match the link; the linked paper (Amazon/AWS researchers, 2024) is about machine-translated sentences in one web translation dataset: 57.1% of the sentences sit in pairs translated into 3+ languages. It is not a measure of AI-generated images, and not \"all online material\".","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Pinterest's only statement on scale: a spokesperson said AI content is \"a small percentage\" of impressions, with no number (via Sherwood News quoting Futurism, 2025-04).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"No independent measurement of the AI share of Pinterest search results was found. The closest is a newsletter spot check (Garbage Day for Sherwood, 2025-04): AI images in the top 20 for every top-10 keyword in every category Pinterest reports. A spot check, not a sample.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Glaze (University of Chicago SAND Lab; USENIX Security 2023) adds near-invisible \"style cloaks\" so a model fine-tuned on the art fails to copy the style. The authors' own tests: >92% of mimicry disrupted normally, >85% against countermeasures; user studies with 1,000+ artists. These are the makers' figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Nightshade (same team; IEEE S&P 2024) is an attack, not a shield: it \"poisons\" images so a model trained on them learns wrong things. In the lab, fewer than 100 poisoned images corrupted one prompt in Stable Diffusion SDXL. The authors pitch it as a last resort against scrapers that ignore opt-outs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Outside test (Hönig, Rando, Carlini, Tramèr, arXiv 2024-06, revised 2025-02): cheap off-the-shelf tricks such as upscaling strip the protections; a user study found all existing protections easily bypassed.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The Glaze team's reply: the attack had significant impact on Glaze v1 and some on v2.0; they shipped 2.1 as stronger, and argue removal costs image quality and works best on flat, smooth styles like anime and cartoons.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"A second attack, LightShed (TU Darmstadt, Cambridge, UT San Antonio; USENIX Security 2025), detects and removes the protections; it spotted Nightshade images 99.98% of the time. University press release.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Both tools' own makers say they are not permanent.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Uptake: \"millions of downloads\" (outside researchers); \"over 8.8 million\" for Glaze and Nightshade together (LightShed's page, citing another source). Downloads, not users.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Don't tag your work\" (the comment advice): no source measures whether tags change what gets scraped. Advice, not a checkable fact.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The Indie Game Awards took back Game of the Year and Debut Game from Clair Obscur: Expedition 33 after its developer, Sandfall, confirmed generative-AI art in production on the ceremony day (2025-12-18). The organisers' statement, as quoted by Engadget (2025-12-22); the awards' own page did not render for us.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The \"form answer\": Sandfall had agreed at submission that no generative AI was used, and the awards' FAQ makes any generative-AI use \"strictly ineligible\". Both via Engadget; the FAQ's eligibility tab was not readable here.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"What the AI was: placeholder textures, later removed, a few of which shipped. The awards went to the runners-up (Blue Prince, Sorry We're Closed).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Larian: a Bloomberg interview (2025-12-16) was read as Larian \"pushing hard\" on generative AI. Its CEO, Swen Vincke, replied the same day on X: 72 artists, 23 of them concept artists, still hiring; AI used to explore references and rough early layouts that artists replace. The headcount is his own claim.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Two days later (2025-12-18) he defended evaluating new tools and promised a Q&A with fans.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The climb-down, at the Reddit Q&A (2026-01-09): no generative-AI art in Divinity, none in concept art; Larian's writing director said no text generation touches the writing; any future AI in-game assets only from models trained on data Larian owns. Via CGMagazine; Reddit blocked our fetch.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-20"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Start: 2024-07-26, after more than 18 months of talks, over AI. The union's 2024-09-05 release, republished verbatim (sagaftra.org blocked us).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-21"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Length: 320 days, suspended in June 2025 (TheWrap, already C27).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-22"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The vote: ratified 95.04% to 4.96%, voting closed 2025-07-09. The union's release, republished verbatim.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-23"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Turnout (how many eligible members voted): not in any saved source.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-24"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Who was struck: the companies signed to the contract, including Disney, Activision, Insomniac and WB Games (TheWrap). Who it couldn't reach: only games that began production after August 2023 were struck, so titles already in production, such as GTA 6, were exempt (Inverse).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-25"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Other studios kept working with union actors on the union's AI terms: 80 games by 2024-09-05, 120 games from 49 companies by 2024-10-22.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-26"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"What the AI terms say (already checked, C27/C28): informed consent for each use, consent can be suspended during a strike, at least 7.5x scale for real-time generation, a usage report within 90 days. All from secondary summaries.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-27"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Mid-strike flashpoint: on 2025-05-19 the union filed an unfair-labour-practice charge against Llama Productions (an Epic Games company) over an AI-generated Darth Vader voice in Fortnite (AP).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-28"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"How that charge ended: not found.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-29"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"He faced 60 years\" = the three 20-year maximums added up at the 2024 charge (DOJ, 2024-09-04). He never pleaded to those three counts.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-30"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The count he pleaded guilty to (2026-03-19) carried a maximum of five years, as the DOJ states it; he got 18 months (P14).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-31"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"The platforms aren't punished\": the DOJ casts the platforms (Amazon Music, Apple Music, Spotify, YouTube Music) as the defrauded party, alongside musicians, and says he lied to them and dodged their anti-fraud rules. Whether platforms share blame is opinion; none of the three releases discusses it.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-32"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The 2024 charge release described two unnamed partners from 2018: the chief executive of an AI music company, who supplied songs, and a music promoter. Neither the plea nor the sentencing release mentions charges against them (a fact about these releases, not the docket).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-33"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"$10 million\" vs \"$8 million\": the FBI's 2024 quote and the Copyright Office's Part 3 report say $10M; the sentencing release says \"more than $8 million\" and orders $8,091,843.64 forfeited. Use $8M.","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-34"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The latest edition is the Future of Jobs Report 2025, published 2025-01-07; the WEF series page lists nothing newer as of 2026-10-28.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-35"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"It is a forecast from an employer survey: 1,000+ employers representing 14 million+ workers in 55 economies, asked what they expect for 2025–2030.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-36"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Graphic designers are on its fastest-declining list, \"just outside the top 10\", a first. In the 2023 edition they were a growing job. Employers name AI and digital access as the drivers.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-37"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"In the same forecast, UI and UX designers are among the 10 fastest-growing jobs.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-38"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The headline: 170 million jobs created, 92 million displaced, net +78 million, across all jobs worldwide. A forecast, not a count, and not about creative work.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-39"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"US Copyright Office, Part 1 (July 2024): copyright does not protect an artistic style as such, and the Office did not recommend adding style to new law. Guidance, not statute.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-40"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Part 3 (pre-publication, May 2025): style imitation can still count as market harm when judging whether AI training is fair use. It cites the Ghibli-style wave and says it could undercut the studio's licensing.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-41"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Studio Ghibli made no public statement on the 2025 trend (The Decoder, 2025). An absence, from a secondary source.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-42"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"⚠ The viral Miyazaki quote (\"an insult to life itself\") is from a 2016 NHK documentary, reacting to an AI motion demo of a figure dragging itself by its head, not to image generators.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-43"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"⚠ A \"Studio Ghibli cease and desist\" that went viral in March 2025 was fake: the app was made up and the studio denied it (fact-check site Alt News).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-44"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"What the studio's side did do: Japan's content trade body CODA, whose members include Studio Ghibli, asked OpenAI on 2025-10-27 not to train Sora 2 on members' work without permission.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-45"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"OpenAI told CODA on 2026-03-27 it would discontinue Sora 2; CODA called it a milestone but said similar tools remain (CODA, 2026-04-01; its 2026-05-27 statement repeats the training demand for all AI firms).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-46"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"OpenAI's 2025 rule (per The Decoder): studio styles allowed, individual living artists' styles blocked. OpenAI's own page was not readable here.","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-47"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Universal Music (UMG) settled with Udio on 2025-10-29: a \"compensatory\" settlement plus licences, a licensed platform due in 2026, and meanwhile songs kept inside Udio. Company release.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-48"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Warner Music (WMG) settled with Udio on 2025-11-19, with a licensing deal for a 2026 platform (Music Business Worldwide).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-49"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"WMG and Suno, 2025-11-25: a partnership that settled the case; licensed models in 2026 replace the current ones; downloads need a paid account; artists opt in to the use of their name, voice and songs; Suno bought Songkick from WMG. Company release.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-50"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Suno's v6 (2026-09-09) was \"developed with\" WMG, BMG and Believe. Vendor blog.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-51"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"UMG and Sony had not settled with Suno, and Sony not with Udio, as of April 2026 (Digital Music News, relaying the Financial Times).","v":"Approximate: not quoted on air","r":"row-t1-52"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Where those cases stand in October 2026, and whether Udio's licensed platform has launched: not found.","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-53"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Who gets paid from the label deals: the musicians' union (AFM) sued UMG and WMG on 2026-06-05 in New York federal court, saying recordings its members played on were licensed to the AI firms without pay or credit (MBW, quoting the complaint).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-54"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Disney and OpenAI, 2025-12-11: a three-year licence for 200+ animated, masked and creature characters in Sora and ChatGPT Images; no actors' likenesses or voices; a planned $1B Disney stake in OpenAI, subject to closing. Company release.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-55"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"⚠ The deal is over. OpenAI announced on 2026-03-24 that it was shutting Sora down, and Disney walked away from the deal and the $1B investment (Engadget citing The Hollywood Reporter; Disney's statement via Campaign).","v":"Corrected before air","r":"row-t1-56"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Disney and Universal sued Midjourney in June 2025 (\"a bottomless pit of plagiarism\"); Warner Bros. Discovery sued in September 2025; in November 2025 the court sent the case to private mediation, due by 2026-08-19. Secondary (LA Mag, Graphic Policy).","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-57"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The outcome of that mediation, or any ruling: not found (no merits ruling in anything opened).","v":"Unresolved: not used on air","r":"row-t1-58"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The card's \"figures people have heard\": the Society of Authors' quarter of UK illustrators (B22) and \"100,000 film jobs\" (D2-39, D2-40) are already checked; WEF is rows T1-35 to T1-39.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-t1-59"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The Copyright Office's streaming-fraud inquiry has not moved: the Federal Register's own record for FR Doc 2026-20537 still gives comments closing 2026-11-23 and lists no corrections, and no later Federal Register document on streaming fraud exists (a full-text search of documents published since 2026-10-08 for \"streaming fraud\" returns 0). The only other Copyright Office document published since 2026-10-07 is an unrelated proposed rule on circumvention exemptions (2026-21939, 2026-10-28). Rows P19 and P20 stand. Read 2026-10-28.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f1"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The Bartz settlement has not paid out yet, and its status is unchanged from row C9: final approval 2026-07-20, the Effective Date agreed to have passed, and the first round of payments still expected on or before 2026-11-15, for works with no allocation dispute and complete claimant information; other works follow later, held in escrow meanwhile. Read 2026-10-28.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f2"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"Udio's licensed platform has not been announced as launched, as far as we found. Udio's own blog lists nothing newer than its Warner announcement of 2025-11-19. A news summary dated 2026-05-25 says Udio is developing a licensed app called Starstruck, shown in a private webinar, with no launch date disclosed. So row T1-53 stays open: the script says only that the labels now license their catalogues to AI music firms (T1-48 to T1-50), and no line depends on a launch. Read 2026-10-28.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f3"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Fell about eight percent in one [year]: about sixteen thousand fewer, roughly one in thirteen\": 214,260 (May 2024, A2) − 197,830 (May 2025, A1c) = 16,430 fewer; 16,430 ÷ 214,260 = 7.67%; 1 in 13 = 7.69%. Salaried (wage-and-salary) graphic designers only.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f4"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Designers' median pay rose about nine percent before inflation\": hourly median $27.88 (May 2022) to $30.27 (May 2025) = +8.57%; annual $57,990 to $62,960 (A3), rounded.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f5"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Twenty minutes of narration through a commercial AI voice service lists at about a dollar and a half\" against \"a human's asking rate for a finished narration that long is eight hundred to over a thousand dollars\": $0.08 a minute (B8) × 20 = $1.60; the GVAA asking-rate bands for 15–20 and 20–30 minutes, $800–$950 and $950–$1,250 (B21). Spoken as raw output against a finished job (the council card's unlike-measure flag), never as \"the price collapsed\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f6"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"About one in five … up from about one in seven a year earlier, and four in five say it hasn't happened to them\": 21% (2026, B1, B2) and 14% (2025, B3) answering yes to \"Have you ever knowingly lost a job to a synthetic voice?\"; 1 in 5 = 20%, 1 in 7 = 14.3%; No = 79%.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f7"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"About two thousand each on average, by our division … since early twenty twenty-four … over more than two years\": $22,000,000 ÷ 10,400 = $2,115 (B15), a cumulative total over a present-tense headcount; payouts began February 2024 (B16) and the $22M figure is dated 2026-05-22, about 2 years 3 months. The company's own figures.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f8"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"The team-cost estimate, shown on screen as \"$4,900 to $18,900 an episode, our estimate\" and spoken only as \"thousands of dollars an episode, our rough estimate\". Low: research 12 h × $47.50 = $570; script 10 h × $36.98 = $369.80; edit 15 h × $36.26 = $543.90; motion graphics 3 days × $350 = $1,050; thumbnail $18; narrator $700; 6 voices × $250 = $1,500; sound 4 h × $35.16 = $140.64; total $4,892.34. High: 25 h × $60 = $1,500; 20 h × $100 = $2,000; 30 h × $100 = $3,000; 8 days × $800 = $6,400; thumbnail $100; narrator $936; 6 × $614 = $3,684; union health and retirement 20.25% × $4,620 = $935.55; 10 h × $35.16 = $351.60; total $18,907.15. Inputs: rows D1-28 to D1-43. The hours, the six one-hour voice sessions and the choice of roles are our assumptions; the host is not costed; BLS medians are employee rates; union scale is a benchmark, not what a channel must pay; several low-end inputs are vendor reproductions (APPROXIMATE outside this estimate). Never \"measured\", never \"saved\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f9"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"US self-published titles rose about thirty-nine percent in twenty twenty-five, to more than three and a half million\": 38.7% (D2-16), rounded; Bowker credits \"new tools\", not AI.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f10"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Books with substantial AI text were a fifth of the books and about eleven percent of the revenue\": 20% and 11.3% (D2-11), rounded.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f11"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Games disclosing AI went from about a dozen launches a month to over five hundred, about one in three new games\": ~13 a month to ~530 a month (D2-18, D2-19), rounded; one independent blogger's census.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f12"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Writers' monthly jobs fell two percent and their earnings about five\": −2% and −5.2% (A16). \"In a twenty twenty-three working paper, image freelancers' jobs fell about four percent and earnings about nine\": −3.7% and −9.4% (A17). Both rounded.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f13"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Twenty-eight percent of game workers surveyed were laid off in the past two years, a third in the US\": 28% and 33% (A26), the 33% rounded.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f14"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Ran three hundred and twenty days … a contract ratified by ninety-five percent of the votes cast\": 320 days (T1-22); 95.04% to 4.96% (T1-23), rounded; turnout unknown (T1-24), so never \"of members\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f15"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"A stock-photo fund paid photographers about three quarters of a cent per image, in one survey of fifty-eight\": average $0.0078 an image (D2-36), rounded.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f16"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"The census counted under a thousand professional photographers in eighteen fifty, and about twenty thousand by eighteen ninety\": 938 and 20,040 (D2-41, D2-42), rounded as the row allows.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f17"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"The most common answer for what a synthetic voice earned in twenty twenty-five was zero\": the largest single group (88 respondents) reported $0 (B28). \"Most common\", never \"most\".","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f18"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"Nearly everyone in its blind test couldn't tell\": 97% (P13), from Deezer's commissioned survey.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f19"},{"s":"ep49-ai-and-creative-jobs","n":49,"c":"\"The count he did plead to carried a maximum of five years\" (T1-31), said against \"sixty years\", which added up the three 20-year maximums at the 2024 charge, counts he never pleaded to (T1-30). And \"in January twenty twenty-five, by its count, it was about one in ten\" is Deezer's \"around 10%\" of daily deliveries (D2-2), as stated.","v":"Confirmed","r":"row-f20"}]);